<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Attalus I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">269–<a href="/years/197-bce">197 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Attalus I Soter</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Attalus I Soter ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> from 241 to 197 BCE. After defeating the Galatians, he took the title of king, the first of his dynasty to do so, and celebrated the victory with monuments that included statues of dying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a>, beginning the partnership that made the Attalid kingdom Rome's chief friend in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/attalus-i.jpg" alt="Attalus I" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergey Sosnovskiy from Saint-Petersburg, Russia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_Hellenistic_ruler_(Attalus_I%3F)._Berlin,_Pergamon_Museum._(5256233048).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Attalus's early life. He was the son of Attalus and Antiochis. His father was a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philetaerus">Philetaerus</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalid dynasty</a>, and won fame as a charioteer at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a>. His mother was probably related to the Seleucid royal family. His father died before 241 BCE, and Attalus was adopted by the ruling dynast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-i">Eumenes I</a>, whom he succeeded on his death in that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Galatians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> who had settled in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, had long exacted tribute from the region's rulers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-i">Eumenes I</a> had probably paid it. Attalus refused, and around 238–235 BCE he defeated them decisively at the Battle of the Caecus River. He then took the surname Soter, meaning saviour, and declared himself king, which formally severed any tie to the Seleucid king as overlord. The victory was commemorated by a monument on the acropolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> that included the sculpture The Dying Gaul.</p><p>In the First Macedonian War Attalus was allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and he took the island of Aegina as his base. In 205 BCE Rome, whose only friend in Asia he was, asked his help in bringing the cult of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Great Mother</a> to Rome, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> he handed over a sacred stone. In 201 BCE he fought a large naval battle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> near Chios and escaped capture by spreading royal treasures on the decks of his stranded ships. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a> he took Andros in 199 BCE and was honoured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, which named a tribe after him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In early 197 BCE, while addressing a Boeotian council at Thebes, Attalus collapsed with one side of his body paralysed. He was taken back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> and died around the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae">Battle of Cynoscephalae</a>, at the age of 72. His kingdom was hardly bigger than at the start of his reign, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> had seized much <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamenes">Pergamene</a> territory. But he had made Pergamon a great centre of art and learning and earned Roman respect. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> notes that he settled the succession of his four sons so well that the crown passed to his children's children without a dispute. His eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Attalus I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of Nicomedes I of Bithynia and settled in the region later named Galatia after them; the victories of <b>Attalus I</b> of Pergamon over the Galatians were celebrated with statues such as the Dying Gaul, and in the 4th century CE Saint Jerome wrote that the Galatians still spoke…</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome captured it in 199 BCE and handed it to <b>Attalus I</b> of the Kingdom of Pergamon, and it passed to direct Roman control after 133 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/andros">Andros</a></li><li><blockquote>After years of raiding western Anatolia, they were defeated by Antiochus I and <b>Attalus I</b> of the Kingdom of Pergamon and settled on the central plateau around Ancyra.</blockquote><a href="/state/galatia">Galatia</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Attalus I</b> took the title of king around 238 BCE after defeating the Galatians.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-pergamon">Kingdom of Pergamon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.12,27.18&amp;year=-223" aria-label="Pergamon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Attalus I"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4813 4221h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4813 4221h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.12,27.18&amp;year=-223">What was here in 223 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
